Revision history for Database-BI

0.014.0	Mon Oct 20 12:00:00 AM EDT 2026
	[ New Features ]
	- Location-column geocoding groundwork:
	  Database::BI::Model::Geocode resolves the distinct values of a place
	  column with Geo::Coder::Free - one lookup per value however often the
	  column repeats it - and skips rather than fails on the blanks, bare
	  numbers and references a real column holds.  Each value is put to the
	  geocoder's valid_location() before it reaches a backend, and the batch is
	  made with on_error => 'undef', so one dirty value cannot end the request.
	  Answers are remembered in the model store (.bi/models.json), a
	  looked-up-and-not-found place is remembered as tried so it is not
	  retried every request, and new lookups are capped per request
	  (map_geocode_max).  Geo::Coder::Free 0.47 or later is an optional
	  dependency: without it the location option is reported as unavailable
	  and the places already resolved are still plotted.
	- GET /map renders the filtered view on an OpenStreetMap map.  With
	  loc=<column> a dataset that has places but no coordinates is mapped by
	  resolving the column's distinct values with
	  Database::BI::Model::Geocode: one lookup per value however often the
	  column repeats it, answers remembered in the dataset's model store so a
	  second visit asks nothing, and new lookups capped per request.  With
	  lat= and lon= the coordinate columns already in the data are plotted
	  instead.  Rows sharing a position become one marker whose popup counts
	  them, rows with no usable coordinate are skipped and reported rather
	  than plotted at (0,0), and a coordinate column wholly out of range is a
	  400 naming it.  Markers are capped per request (map_max_markers), popup
	  columns are chosen with popup=, and HTML::OSM and Geo::Coder::Free are
	  both optional: without the renderer the page says so, and without the
	  geocoder the places already remembered are still plotted.
	[ Enhancements ]
	- The "geocoder unavailable" message shown on a map page is no longer
	  printed twice: geocoder_available() returns the finished sentence and
	  the result reuses it instead of formatting it again.
	- The location counts follow Geo::Coder::Free 0.48: its geocode_many()
	  lookups is now the number of reads the backends made, so a value one of
	  their process-level caches answered is not counted.  counts->lookups
	  therefore reports work avoided as well as work done, and is never more
	  than the values attempted.  The count is still the geocoder's own
	  rather than the app's; only its meaning for a warm process is finer.
	- Canvas resize handles are keyboard operable and exposed to assistive
	  technology).  Each card's handle is now in the tab order and
	  carries slider semantics: left and right change the width in grid
	  columns, up and down change the height in rows, and Home and End jump to
	  the narrowest and widest the card can be.  The size is announced as it
	  changes ("6 columns wide, 2 rows high"), the focused card is outlined so
	  it is clear which visual the keys will resize, and a keyboard resize is
  saved exactly like a drag - once, after the last key press, and at once
  if focus leaves the handle first.
	- Chart resize handles get the same treatment as the canvas ones.  The
	  corner grip on every chart page (line, pie, bar, heatmap, scatter,
	  stacked, combo, treemap, histogram) is in the tab order and carries
	  slider semantics: left and right change the width, up and down the
	  height, Shift takes bigger steps and Home and End go to the narrowest and
	  widest the chart can be - the content width of the box the chart is drawn
	  in, measured from the container's own left edge, so the corner grip stays
	  on screen however far in the page indents it.  The size is announced as
	  it changes ("640 pixels wide, 480 pixels high"), the focused grip is drawn at
	  full strength with the chart outlined so it is clear which one the keys resize,
	  and #chart-resize-help describes the keys.  The size is remembered: one key
	  per kind of chart (bi:chart-size:<path>), so changing a filter does not
	  lose it and a shared link opens at the recipient's own size rather than
	  the sender's.  It is written when a keyboard press lands and once when a
	  drag is let go - not on every pointer move - and read back before the grip
	  is created, so the grip opens reporting the size the chart is drawn at.
	  A stored size is clamped to the window it is restored in, and a value that
	  is not two numbers is ignored rather than applied; storage that throws
	  leaves the grip working, it just forgets the size.
	- A remembered chart size can be forgotten instead of dragged back.  Every
	  chart page's export bar now carries a Reset size button: it removes the
	  page's bi:chart-size:<path> entry - rather than overwriting it with the
	  default - and puts each chart on the page back at the size the server sent,
	  so the next visit asks the server again.  The button is a visible control in
	  the tab order rather than a key a reader has to know, its tooltip says what
	  it forgets, and #chart-resize-help names it for a reader who cannot see it.
	  The size the reset returns to is the one captured before a saved size is
	  applied, and the announcement ("Chart size reset to 480 by 320 pixels.")
	  goes to a polite live region.  The resize wrap now goes around the SVG
	  wherever it sits: HTML::D3 nests the pie in #pie_chart_wrap, so inserting
	  it into the outer .pie-container threw and left the pie page with neither
	  a grip nor the Reset size control.
	- Canvas visuals are dragged to a chosen grid cell, not just resized and
	  reordered).  A card's header is the grab area: the card snaps
	  to the cell the pointer is over, a ghost cell shows where it will land,
	  and on release the card is placed there while anything sharing that cell
	  is pushed down out of the way - only cards that collide move, so a card
	  in row 4 stays in row 4 with a gap above it, because a canvas is a place
	  and  not a list.  The header is also a control: it takes focus, the arrow
	  keys move the card a cell at a time, Shift with an arrow key goes to that
	  edge of the canvas, and each move is announced - the cell the card landed
	  in, and by name any card the move pushed down, so a reader who is told
	  only about the card they moved does not lose track of the rest.  The whole
	  layout - x, y, width and height - is persisted, and PATCH
	  /api/analyses/:id rejects a layout in which two visuals share a cell,
	  leaving the saved one alone.
	- A card's width and height can be set from a menu on the card rather than
	  by dragging its corner).  Every card on a canvas carries a Size menu whose
	  entries are widths in grid columns - full width, half, a third, a quarter -
	  and heights in grid rows - one, two, three or six - so a visual can be
	  sized in both dimensions from the keyboard alone; it is a native select,
	  because that is operable and announced on every platform without any code
	  from us, and because a card clips its own contents and a panel built in the
	  header would be cut off at the card's edge.  The size that is chosen is the
	  size that lands: a card in column 9 slides left to be twelve columns wide
	  rather than being quietly cut to four, a height keeps the width that was
	  there just as a width keeps the height, and whatever it grows over is
	  pushed down, so what is saved is a layout the server accepts - an
	  overlapping layout is refused, which a drag widening over a neighbour could
	  produce until now, so the drag and the arrow keys resolve it the same way
	  as the menu does.  The menu's first option says the size the card actually
	  is - "Half width, 2 rows high", or "7 columns wide, 3 rows high" after a
	  drag - and is what the menu shows whenever the size came from a drag, an
	  arrow key or a reload; a choice made in the menu itself stays selected, so
	  the arrow keys walk the entries one after another instead of being pulled
	  back to the top.  Focus stays on the menu after a choice, and the new size
	  is saved and announced as it is made.  A card the new size has to push down
	  is named in that announcement as well.
	- The browser tests share a fixture and run as one suite.  t/lib/BI/Test/Browser.pm
	  starts the app on an ephemeral port with a throwaway model_dir, a headless
	  Firefox with its own profile and a Marionette connection, and presses keys
	  through the browser's input stack; t/lib/BI/Test/Marionette.pm is the
	  protocol client it speaks.  t/browser-size.t, the Size menu test the
	  fixture grew out of, still shows one key press changing the card's
	  computed grid-column span and sending the layout PATCH, which the app
	  records before the request is routed, so the test sees the browser's
	  request rather than one of its own.  The suite runs as
	  `prove -l t/browser-*.t` and skips when no Firefox is available.
	- t/browser-canvas-keys.t drives the canvas's three keyboard controls with
	  real key presses: the header handle's arrow keys and Shift+arrow edges,
	  the corner grip's width, height, Home and End, and the Size menu's
	  typeahead and arrow keys.  Each press is read back from the browser - the
	  computed grid position and span, the size on the card, the menu's summary,
	  the grip's slider attributes and the live region - and the layout the app
	  stored is asserted after each burst of keys, so a move that is shown but
	  not saved fails.
	- t/browser-chart-keys.t follows a chart from the table view's toolbar to
	  its export bar with the keyboard alone: Enter opens the Bar chart panel,
	  native typeahead picks the category and enables Plot, Tab walks the
	  panel's controls, Enter opens the chart, the arrow keys resize it through
	  its grip (Shift, Home and End included), and Tab then Enter export it as
	  SVG and PNG, with Space copying its link.  The exports are observed
	  through the page's own Blob and anchor, and the SVG's text is read back,
	  so each control is shown to have produced the file it names.  It then
	  leaves the chart at a size the server does not send - first with the
	  keyboard, then by dragging the grip with real pointer actions - opens the
	  page again under a different query, and asserts that the SVG, the grip's
	  value and the text a screen reader is given all report the restored size.
	- t/browser-chart-a11y.t tests the chart grip's accessibility contract on
	  its own, in headless Firefox: the slider role, the label naming the
	  chart, the instructions it points at, the announced minimum and maximum,
	  and a Tab from the top of the page that lands on the grip rather than a
	  script that puts focus there.  Then each key - Home, the arrows, Shift,
	  End - is pressed and the SVG's drawn size is read back against
	  aria-valuenow and the aria-valuetext a screen reader speaks, so a size
	  that changes without being announced, or an announcement that drifts
	  from the drawing, fails; pressing past either limit leaves the announced
	  value truthful, and no press scrolls the page.
	- The keyboard suite is a CI job of its own.  .github/workflows/browser.yml
	  installs Mozilla's Firefox ESR tarball rather than using the runner's
	  snap (a snap's private /tmp cannot see the profile the fixture writes),
	  and runs t/browser-*.t with real key presses.  BI_REQUIRE_BROWSER=1 makes a
	  missing or broken browser fail the job rather than skip every test, so
	  the workflow cannot be green with nothing run.  The Perl matrix sets
	  BI_SKIP_BROWSER_TESTS=1 and leaves this to that job: a browser test says
	  nothing about which Perl ran it, and one browser is the honest check.
	  JSON::MaybeXS, which the Marionette client decodes its frames with, is a
	  declared test prerequisite now that a clean checkout runs the client.
	- A canvas says what its two grips are for the first time a reader opens
	  one, and never again).  The tip is rendered hidden and
	  uncovered by the script, so a reader who has already dismissed it does
	  not get a flash of it on the way in; the answer is kept in localStorage
	  under a key of the page's own, because what the tip explains -- the
	  header drag and the corner grip -- belongs to the canvas page and not to
	  one analysis, so a second canvas is not a second first visit.  It closes
	  with a "Got it" button or Escape, either of which hides the tip, writes
	  the answer and leaves focus on the header handle it named; while it is
	  up, the header and the grip it describes are ringed and tinted on the
	  first card, because a name in a sentence is not a thing on the screen.
	  A canvas with no cards says nothing, a browser that cannot store the
	  answer shows the tip and forgets it at the end of the visit, and the tip
	  does not reach paper.
	- The arrangement a canvas was given now survives a cross-filter and a
	  drill-through visit.  A visit renders the layout in the analysis, so the
	  cards come back in the cells they were left in whatever the filter leaves
	  them to show, and a card the canvas has not placed itself is left to the
	  browser instead of being measured into the layout: writing every card's
	  position from the render in hand is what would freeze the filtered
	  arrangement as if it were the one the canvas was given, and a card
	  measured on a narrow filtered render could land on a cell another card
	  already had.  Nothing is saved by a visit either -- the layout is written
	  only when a reader moves or resizes a card -- and a press on the corner
	  grip that does not move it is no longer a resize that rewrites every
	  position.  A layout the server refuses (two cards in one cell) is
	  announced rather than swallowed, so a size that did not save is a size
	  the reader knows to set again.
	- t/browser-size.t opens the same canvas as a cross-filtered visit and as a
	  drill path, and reads back the span the browser laid out and the size the
	  menu reports for each, so both are shown to be the saved ones; neither
	  visit may PATCH a layout of its own, which is asserted from the requests
	  the app recorded.  t/model.t asserts the same server-side: the rendered
	  grid position of every card is unchanged by a cross-filter or a drill
	  visit, and the stored layout is not rewritten by either.
	- A card's size is written on the card (3.2), in its header beside the Size
	  menu: the same two numbers the menu's first option spells out in words, a
	  card ten columns wide and two rows high reading "10 × 2".  A drag of the
	  corner grip can leave any width at all, and that width was reported in one
	  place only - inside the menu, whose text is the one thing on the card that
	  shrinks when the card is narrow.  The number on the card is columns and
	  rows in full, with the menu's own words for the size ("Half width, 2 rows
	  high") as its title, and it is written by the template from the saved
	  spans, so a reload shows it with no JavaScript involved.  The script
	  writes it at load for a card the browser placed rather than the canvas, on
	  every resize - a drag updates it as the grip moves, not only when it is let
	  go - and whenever the
	  menu sets a size: the menu's first option and the number on the card are
	  written through one function, so no path can leave one of them claiming a
	  size the other disagrees with.  A card with no size yet - one the browser
	  has not laid out - renders an empty badge, and :empty is what keeps that
	  from being a small blank pill of padding rather than nothing; the badge does
	  not shrink where the menu it sits beside does, and it is not printed, since
	  the printed canvas is two columns whatever the canvas is and the number
	  would describe a page that is not there.
	- The controls that were still reachable with a pointer only now answer the
	  keyboard as well, audited page by page.  A pivot table's column headers
	  sort and move: each leaf header is in the tab order, Enter and Space sort
	  by it - the key a click already performs - Shift+Left and Shift+Right move
	  it where a drag is offered, and a polite live region says which column was
	  sorted and which way, or where a moved column landed, because neither
	  gesture moves the focus.  A two-row header (a column dimension) refuses
	  the move: shifting a measure column out from under its group label would
	  leave the label over another group's numbers.  #pivot-head-help describes
	  both routes, and the focused header is ringed on the dark header it sits
	  on.
	- A home-page card showed its file's type, size and date in a tooltip on
	  hover, so a keyboard reader tabbing the same cards was told nothing.  All
	  three card lists - the data files, the recently opened and saved entries,
	  and the saved canvases - now show that tip on focus and hide it on blur,
	  and each card is described by its own tip (role="tooltip", with a polite
	  live region for the size the lazy /api/stat fetch adds after it is shown),
	  so tabbing to a card says what a pointer reader is shown.  The trash-can
	  button on those cards is a real button in the tab order but was drawn only
	  while its list item was hovered, which left it focusable while invisible:
	  it is now drawn at full strength when it has focus and at hover strength
	  whenever focus is anywhere in the item.
	- Chart legends toggle a series on click and were pointer-only.  The entries
	  of a stacked chart's legend, and of a multi-series line chart's, are now
	  switches: in the tab order, named "Toggle <series>", aria-pressed saying
	  whether that series is drawn, Enter or Space sending the same click the
	  pointer sends, and #stacked-legend-help / #line-legend-help describing the
	  keys.  The series appearing or going is announced by a live region as
	  well, since a pressed state on the entry is not heard by every screen
	  reader, and the focused entry is ringed on its swatch, because a <g> has
	  no box of its own.  A single-series line chart has no legend and is given
	  no instructions for one.
	- A treemap tile's value - the area the tile is drawn at - cannot be read
	  out, so the value is now part of the tile's name ("Filter table to
	  Widget A (1250)") and the tooltip D3 shows on hover is shown on focus too,
	  positioned from the tile rather than from a pointer.
	- t/model.t covers each of those controls server-side: the pivot headers'
	  keys, refusal and announcements; the three card lists' focus handlers and
	  descriptions; the legends' switches; and the treemap's named value.  Two
	  browser tests press real keys for the parts a source check cannot reach:
	  t/browser-pivot-keys.t sorts a column with Enter, reverses it, sorts by
	  another with Space, moves a column with Shift+Left/Right and asserts that
	  the same keys leave a two-row header where it was; t/browser-home-keys.t
	  checks that focusing a card shows its tip with the fetched modified date,
	  that Tab hides it again, and that the hover-only remove button is drawn
	  when focus is inside its list item.  The audit's two remaining
	  pointer-only gestures are documented in the README: brushing a line chart
	  to zoom (its Reset zoom button is a keyboard control) and dropping a file
	  on the window to upload it (typing the path, or Browse, is the keyboard
	  route).  Every other element that behaves as a control is now in the tab
	  order and answers the keyboard.
	[ Interface Fixes ]
	- GET /combo now accepts ?bar_val= and ?line_val= as the canonical parameter
	  names (as documented in CLAUDE.md and the POD).  The short forms ?bar= and
	  ?line= remain as backwards-compatible aliases so existing saved URLs continue
	  to work.  The combo panel in the dashboard submits the canonical names.
	- GET /combo now accepts ?cat= as the canonical x-axis parameter (?x= remains
	  a backwards-compatible alias), consistent with /stacked and the panel JS.
	[ Bug Fixes ]
	- A canvas card's size could be lost with nothing said: saveLayout() only
	  handled a transport failure, so a layout the server refused (400, two
	  cards in one cell) left the size on screen and nowhere else, silently.
	  The refused and unreachable cases are both announced now.
	- The data_dir guard on the app-owned stores could be walked around with a
	  symlink: a model_dir that reached data_dir through a link -- or a data_dir
	  that was itself a link, with the store naming the directory it resolved to
	  -- was compared as a string and allowed through, so models.json and
	  analyses.json were written beside the user's data.  Both store paths now
	  compare symlink-resolved paths, resolving the deepest existing ancestor
	  when the store does not exist yet, and ask the file system what the
	  relationship between the two is rather than testing a textual prefix; a
	  store inside data_dir is refused however it is spelled.

0.013.0 Fri Oct 16 04:08:48 PM EDT 2026
	[ New Features ]
	- Saved canvases: multi-visual workspace pages (/a/:id) that combine
	  charts and table views.  Full CRUD API at /api/analyses.  Canvas
	  pages support cross-filtering, drill-through hierarchies,
	  parameterisation (?p.<name>=), bookmarks with undo/redo, per-visual
	  PNG download, and print to PDF/paper.
	[ Enhancements ]
	- Chart resize: all chart pages (line, pie, bar, heatmap, scatter,
	  stacked, combo, treemap, histogram) now show a drag handle at the
	  bottom-right corner of the chart SVG.  Dragging it resizes the chart
	  proportionally without a page reload or D3 re-render.

0.012.1	Thu Oct 15 12:00:00 PM EDT 2026
    [ Bug Fixes ]
    - Fix 'Argument "" isn't numeric in int' warnings logged to the console
      when numeric query parameters (?topn=, ?max=, ?max_cols=, ?per_page=,
      ?page=, ?min_r=, ?max_r=) are submitted with an empty value.
    - Fix ?bins=0 reverting to 10 bins instead of clamping to 1: the
      || 10 empty-string guard must not swallow the literal value 0.
      histogram_view now uses the defined-and-non-empty pattern so that
      ?bins=0 correctly passes through the $bins < 1 clamp.
    [ Tests ]
    - Add t/binned.t: end-to-end contract tests for /histogram pre-binned
      mode (label/count correctness, bins=N clamping, max-lands-in-top-bin,
      no d3.bin() call, bin()/histogram label agreement, virtual column
      binning, flat fixture, empty/error state).

0.012.0	Wed Oct 14 09:10:15 PM EDT 2026
    [ New Features ]
    - GET /pivot: crosstab/pivot table.  Accepts ?rows=, ?cols=, and
      ?m=col:fn:alias (sum|avg|min|max|count|count_distinct); ?totals=1
      adds subtotals and a grand-total row; ?h= applies post-aggregation
      HAVING filters; ?topn=N+?topn_col= keeps the top-N row groups;
      ?max_cols=N collapses tail column combos into "Other";
      ?format=csv|sqlite|json|xlsx exports the result.
    - GET /stats: per-column statistics (row count, null count, distinct
      count, min, max, mean, median).
    - GET /scatter: scatter/bubble plot (?x=, ?y=, ?series=, ?size=,
      ?scheme=, ?min_r=, ?max_r=).  The scatter panel now exposes all
      parameters: "Colour by" (any column), "Bubble size" (numeric
      columns only), colour scheme (tableau10|category10|set2|set3|paired
      -- enabled only when a series column is chosen), and Min/Max radius
      1-40 (enabled only when a size column is chosen).  ?scheme= is
      validated server-side; an unrecognised name returns 400.
    - GET /stacked: stacked bar chart (?cat=, ?series=, ?val=).
    - GET /combo: combo bar+line chart (?cat=, ?bar_val=, ?line_val=).
    - GET /treemap: treemap (?group=, ?size=, ?topn=N).
    - GET /histogram: histogram with server-side binning (?col=, ?bins=N;
      default 10 bins; max 200).
    - GET /line: alias for GET /graph (all /graph params accepted).
    - GET /graph: new ?series=<col> parameter for multi-series line charts.
    - Calculated fields (Database::BI::Calc): recursive-descent evaluator
      supporting arithmetic, comparison, IF, string, numeric, and date
      functions; bin(); coalesce; isnull.  All chart and pivot routes
      accept ?c=name:expr (repeatable).  Stored fields managed via
      GET /calc/list, POST /calc/save, POST /calc/delete.
    - Date normalisation: stored date-format patterns convert non-ISO
      columns to ISO-8601 before aggregation.  Managed via
      GET /dates/list, POST /dates/save, POST /dates/delete.
    - Named value groups: virtual "${col}_group" columns map raw values
      to analyst-defined labels.  Managed via GET /groups/list,
      POST /groups/save, POST /groups/delete.
    - Redis backend: DataSource accepts redis => 'redis://...' and the
      redis:tablename|redis://... spec format.

    [ Enhancements ]
    - GET /join and GET /combine now support pagination (?page=N,
      ?per_page=M, ?sort=<col>, ?dir=asc|desc).
    - GET /export and POST /export now accept ?page=N and ?per_page=M.
    - f= filters and d=1 dedup applied before pagination so the N+1
      row count reflects the reduced set (reduce-before-paginate).
    - bar_view and pie_view: new ?topn=N parameter; remainder collapsed
      into "Other".
    - Added X-BI-Warnings response header to GET /export.
    - All chart render() calls now use a unified chart_html stash key
      and include a chart => { kind, title, encode, options } DTO.
    - Added DataSource::dbi_source() for SQL-capability detection and
      Database::Join zero-copy ATTACH path.
    - Added _grouped_totals() as the single aggregation entry point for
      all chart routes, with SQL push-down and Perl fold fallback.

    [ Bug Fixes ]
    - /join and /combine: no-params default now returns the first 200
      rows with a pagination bar, consistent with /view and /open.
    - f= filters on paginated views no longer produce short pages.
    - d=1 dedup now deduplicates the full result before slicing.
    - has_next recomputed after in-memory filter/dedup reduction.
    - Chart category order no longer changes when a filter is applied:
      Perl fold now returns groups in the same ascending key order as
      the SQL path.
    - Aggregation on SQLite sources pushes SQL-compatible f= filters to
      the database; non-SQL-able filters still fall back to Perl fold.
    - DataSource::dbi_source() no longer propagates exceptions from
      non-SQL backends; returns undef instead.
    - CSV and other non-SQL sources no longer emit "Bad limit clause"
      warnings; LIMIT/OFFSET is only sent to SQL-backed sources.

    [ Dependencies ]
    - HTML::D3 >= 0.20 (radius_range support for scatter bubble charts;
      0.19 added the five new snippet methods).
    - Database::Abstraction >= 0.51 (redis key support).
    - Database::Join >= 0.009.0 (aggregate, limit/offset, dbi_source).

0.011.0	Sun Oct 11 09:20:48 PM EDT 2026
    [ Enhancements ]
    - Added schema browser (GET /tables) for multi-table SQLite databases.
      Opening a SQLite file with more than one internal table now shows a
      card grid listing every table with its columns rather than silently
      opening the first table.  Clicking a card opens that table via the
      existing /open view.  The schema page is inserted into the breadcrumb
      trail so users can navigate back to it.
    - Added ?it=<table> parameter to GET /open, allowing a specific internal
      table to be opened from a multi-table SQLite file.
    - Added sqlite_tables() accessor to DataSource, returning the list of
      internal table names discovered during construction.
    - Added internal_table constructor option to DataSource, letting callers
      request a specific internal SQLite table instead of the auto-detected
      first match.
    - Added data/store.db: sample SQLite database with three joinable tables
      (customer, product, sales) for development and demonstration.
    - Fixed: multi-table symlink for .db-extension SQLite files now uses
      .sql as the symlink extension so Database::Abstraction opens it via
      the DBI/SQLite backend rather than falling through to the CSV reader.
    NOTE: /tables tests currently cover SQLite only.  MySQL, PostgreSQL,
    and other multi-table driver tests will be added in a future release
    once the corresponding Database::Abstraction drivers are available.
    - Server-side pagination for /view/:table and /open.  Both routes now accept
      ?page=N (1-based, default 1) and ?per_page=M (default 200, max 2000).
      The controller uses the N+1 trick: it requests per_page+1 rows via
      LIMIT/OFFSET and treats the extra row as a "has next page" sentinel rather
      than issuing a COUNT(*) query.  This is critical for multi-GB SQLite files
      (e.g. openaddresses.db) where COUNT(*) would block the server for minutes.
      A pagination bar (Prev / current page / Next) is shown when at least two
      pages exist.  All URL parameters (filters, dedup, per_page, etc.) are
      preserved across page navigations.
    - BerkeleyDB files larger than 100 MB now return an informative error page
      instead of causing disk thrashing.  Files whose magic bytes identify them as
      BerkeleyDB but whose DB_File tie() fails (e.g. Berkeley DB version 9 files
      opened with libdb-5.3) would previously fall through to D::A's CSV path,
      causing a full read of the binary file.  DataSource now detects the magic
      bytes in _detect_file_info, refuses files above 100 MB immediately, and
      returns error_unsupported_db for smaller files where tie() fails.
    - Three-level breadcrumb for chart pages.  When a bar, line-graph, pie,
      or heatmap chart is launched from a table view that was itself reached
      from the schema browser (/tables), the chart page now shows the full
      navigation chain (e.g. Home > Store schema > Table view > Chart) rather
      than the previous two-level "Home > Back to table".  The dashboard
      panel forms forward the current page's back_url as back2=/back2_label=
      parameters; the chart controllers promote them to the outer breadcrumb
      level so all four levels are rendered correctly.
    - SQLite hover tooltip on the home-page file picker.  Hovering over a
      database card in the "Choose a database" list now shows a dark popover
      with the file type (e.g. "SQLite database - 3 tables"), last-modified
      date, and file size.  GET /api/stat now returns a table_count integer
      for SQLite-extension files (.sql, .sqlite, .sqlite3, .db); the count
      is omitted for Berkeley DB .db files or other non-SQLite payloads.
      The home-page db-cards now carry data-path attributes so the JS fetch
      can call /api/stat on first mouseenter (lazy, not on page load).

0.010.0	Tue Oct  6 11:29:33 AM EDT 2026
    [ Enhancements ]
    - Added Folder view (GET /folder): browse any data file grouped by the
      letter prefix of a chosen code column (e.g. Order # values like A10,
      CT4, B3).  Records are organised into a two- or three-level folder
      hierarchy -- top level shows distinct first letters (A-Z), clicking a
      letter reveals sub-prefix folders (e.g. CA, CB, CC inside C) and any
      items with that exact prefix, clicking a sub-prefix shows the matching
      records in a table.  A "Folder view..." toolbar button opens a column-
      picker panel identical in style to the chart panels; the code column is
      auto-detected when the panel is opened but the user can override it via
      the dropdown.  Breadcrumb navigation lets the user step back to any
      folder level or return to the source table via "Back to table".
    - upload_file now guards move_to() with eval { ...; 1 } and checks -f
      $dest after the call.  A failed save returns a JSON 500 with the key
      error_upload_save instead of propagating an unhandled exception.
      The orphaned upload subdirectory is removed before the error response
      is sent.  (Uses the eval { ...; 1 } idiom to avoid false positives
      from a stale $@ left by Mojolicious internal dispatch code.)

    - All chart pages (/graph, /pie, /heatmap, /bar) now use responsive SVG
      layout (HTML::D3 0.18 `responsive => 1`): charts scale to the available
      container width so they render correctly on mobile and narrow windows.
    - /bar: added ?max=N parameter -- when N > 0, bars beyond the top N are
      collapsed into a single "Other" bar (HTML::D3 `max_bars`).  Prevents
      unreadable charts from high-cardinality categorical columns.
    - /bar: category-column name is now shown as an axis label below the bars
      (HTML::D3 `x_label`).
    - /pie: added ?scheme= parameter to select the D3 categorical colour
      palette (tableau10, category10, set2, set3, paired; default tableau10).
      Mirrors the existing ?scheme= parameter already on /heatmap.
    - /join: added ?jtype=left|inner|outer parameter to control join semantics.
      `left` (default) keeps all rows from the left table; `inner` returns
      only rows that match in both; `outer` returns all rows from either table.
      Exposed in the "Merge data" panel as a "Which rows to include" dropdown.
    - /join: schema-type-mismatch warnings from Database::Join (e.g. "column
      'score' has type TEXT in left table but INTEGER in right table") are now
      captured and displayed as a yellow notice bar above the data table,
      instead of landing silently in the server error log.
    - DataSource now implements updated() -- proxies to Database::Abstraction's
      updated() or stats the file directly.  This allows Database::Join to
      invalidate its SQLite spill cache when a source file changes on disk.

